<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ole Rømer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1644">1644</a>–<a href="/years/1710">1710</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ole Roemer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ole Rømer was a Danish astronomer who in 1676, working at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Observatory, showed from the timing of eclipses of Jupiter's moon Io that light travels at a finite speed and made the first estimate of it. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> he was royal mathematician and chief of police, and he reformed Danish weights and measures and the calendar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ole-romer.jpg" alt="Ole Rømer" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacob Coning · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%B8mer,_Ole_(ur_Ber%C3%B8mte_danske_maend).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
